[
{ch:17,q:"How is the rate of a chemical reaction defined?",choices:["The change in concentration of a particular reactant or product per unit time","The total mass of reactants converted into products","The time taken for a reaction to reach equilibrium","The number of moles of reactant present at the start of a reaction"],explain:"The rate of a chemical reaction is the increase in concentration of products, or the decrease in concentration of reactants, per unit time."},
{ch:17,q:"What are the standard units of reaction rate expressed in terms of concentration?",choices:["mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>","mol dm<sup>3</sup> s","dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"Since rate is a change in concentration per unit time, its units are mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"A tangent is drawn to a graph of reactant concentration, [R], against time at t = 120 s. The tangent shows a change of &minus;2.40 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> over 283 s. Calculate the rate of reaction at t = 120 s.",choices:["8.48 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","2.40 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","2.83 &times; 10<sup>2</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","5.90 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"rate = 2.40 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> / 283 s = 8.48 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>, reported as positive by convention."},
{ch:17,q:"A tangent drawn at t = 0 s shows a change of &minus;1.92 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> over 67 s, and a tangent at t = 90 s shows a change of &minus;1.18 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> over 164 s. What do these two rates (2.9 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> and 7.2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> respectively) illustrate?",choices:["Rate is greatest at the start of the reaction and decreases sharply as the reaction proceeds","Rate is constant throughout the reaction, regardless of concentration","Rate increases steadily as the reaction proceeds","Rate is independent of the concentration of the reactant"],explain:"The initial rate (2.9 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup>) is roughly four times the rate at 90 s (7.2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>), confirming rate falls sharply as reactant concentration is used up."},
{ch:17,q:"Why is the initial rate (the tangent at t = 0) commonly used to compare the rates of different reactions or conditions?",choices:["Because rate is not constant during a reaction, so the initial rate gives a single, fair point of comparison before concentration has changed","Because the initial rate is always identical for every reaction","Because it is the only point on the graph that can be measured accurately","Because reverse reactions only become significant after the first instant"],explain:"Since rate varies throughout a reaction, the tangent at t = 0 gives a consistent basis for comparing different reactions or different conditions, and is especially useful for investigating the effect of concentration on rate."},
{ch:17,q:"What is the key difference between the instructions &ldquo;draw a graph&rdquo; and &ldquo;sketch a graph&rdquo;?",choices:["Drawing is based on actual data with appropriately chosen scales and clearly marked points, while sketching shows a general trend without reference to specific data","Drawing always uses a curve, while sketching always uses a straight line","Drawing requires no axis labels, while sketching requires fully labelled axes","There is no difference between the two instructions"],explain:"Drawing a graph uses actual data with appropriate scales and marked points; sketching shows a trend without reference to specific data, though both require clearly labelled axes."},
{ch:17,q:"For Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) &rarr; MgCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g), which technique is most convenient for following the rate of reaction?",choices:["Measuring the change in volume of gas produced using a gas syringe or displacement of water","Measuring the change in colour using a colorimeter","Measuring the change in mass, since hydrogen gives a large, easily measured decrease","Titrating samples of the reaction mixture"],explain:"Since this reaction produces hydrogen gas, collecting and measuring its volume is the most convenient method; the mass-loss method is unsuitable since H<sub>2</sub> is too light to give a measurable change in mass."},
{ch:17,q:"When following a reaction by withdrawing samples for titration at intervals, why is a quenching agent added to each sample as soon as it is withdrawn?",choices:["To stop the reaction in that sample immediately, giving a 'freeze-frame' snapshot, since titration itself takes time during which the reaction would otherwise continue","To dissolve any solid precipitate so titration can proceed","To make the sample a distinctive colour so the end point is easier to see","To increase the analyte concentration so a smaller titre is needed"],explain:"Because titration takes time, a quenching agent is added to stop the reaction immediately at the moment of sampling, giving an accurate snapshot of concentration at that instant."},
{ch:17,q:"For BrO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 5Br<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 6H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 3Br<sub>2</sub>(aq) + 3H<sub>2</sub>O(l), which technique is most appropriate for following the rate, and why?",choices:["Measuring the change in electrical conductivity, since the total ionic concentration falls sharply from 12 to 0","Measuring the change in volume of gas produced, since Br<sub>2</sub> is released as a gas","Measuring the change in mass, since the mixture becomes significantly lighter","Using a clock reaction, since no continuous signal is available"],explain:"On the reactant side there are 12 mol of dissolved ions per formula unit, falling to 0 on the product side (Br<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O are uncharged), so conductivity gives a convenient continuous signal."},
{ch:17,q:"In the 'disappearing cross' clock reaction, what is the main limitation of the method compared with a continuous monitoring technique?",choices:["It gives only an average rate over the time interval measured, not the rate at a specific instant","It can only be used with coloured reactants","It requires a spectrophotometer to detect the end point","It cannot be repeated under different conditions"],explain:"A clock reaction uses a single, fixed end point rather than a continuous reading, so it can only give an average rate over the measured interval, unlike the continuous techniques."},
{ch:17,q:"What information about the rate of a reaction can be obtained directly from its balanced chemical equation?",choices:["No information at all; rate can only be determined from experimental (empirical) data","The exact numerical value of the initial rate","The order of reaction with respect to each reactant","The value of the activation energy, E<sub>a</sub>"],explain:"The balanced equation gives no information about rate; this can only be obtained through experimental measurement, since it depends on the reaction mechanism, not the overall stoichiometry."},
{ch:17,q:"According to the kinetic molecular theory, what is temperature in kelvin directly proportional to?",choices:["The average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance","The total kinetic energy of every particle in a substance combined","The average potential energy of the particles in a substance","The number of collisions occurring per second"],explain:"Temperature in kelvin is proportional to the average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance."},
{ch:17,q:"What does the area enclosed under a Maxwell&ndash;Boltzmann energy distribution curve represent?",choices:["The total number of particles in the sample","The average kinetic energy of the particles in the sample","The activation energy of the reaction","The total mass of the sample"],explain:"The area enclosed under the whole Maxwell&ndash;Boltzmann curve represents the total number of particles in the sample."},
{ch:17,q:"Which is the correct definition of activation energy, E<sub>a</sub>?",choices:["The minimum value of kinetic energy that particles must have before they are able to react","The total energy released when a reaction is complete","The average kinetic energy of all particles in a sample at a given temperature","The energy required to heat a sample by 1 kelvin"],explain:"Activation energy is the minimum kinetic energy particles must have before a collision can lead to a successful reaction."},
{ch:17,q:"According to collision theory, which two conditions must be met for a collision to be successful?",choices:["Kinetic energy greater than the activation energy, and correct collision geometry (orientation)","Identical kinetic energy for both particles, and an exothermic reaction","The same temperature for both particles, and high pressure","Gas-phase reactants, and the presence of a catalyst"],explain:"Collision theory requires both sufficient energy (greater than E<sub>a</sub>) and proper orientation for a collision to lead to reaction."},
{ch:17,q:"On a reaction energy profile for an endothermic reaction, where are the products drawn relative to the reactants?",choices:["At a higher potential energy than the reactants, giving a positive &Delta;H","At a lower potential energy than the reactants, giving a positive &Delta;H","At the same potential energy as the reactants, giving &Delta;H = 0","At a higher potential energy than the reactants, giving a negative &Delta;H"],explain:"In an endothermic reaction, products have higher potential energy than reactants, so &Delta;H is positive."},
{ch:17,q:"How does increasing temperature affect a Maxwell&ndash;Boltzmann distribution curve for a fixed sample of gas?",choices:["The peak becomes lower and broader and shifts toward higher kinetic energy, while the total area stays the same","The peak becomes taller and narrower and shifts toward lower kinetic energy","The curve becomes a symmetrical bell shape centred on the activation energy","The area under the curve increases, since particles gain mass at higher temperature"],explain:"At higher temperature, more particles have higher kinetic energy, so the peak becomes lower, broader, and shifts right; the area is unchanged, since it represents the fixed total number of particles."},
{ch:17,q:"Why does increasing temperature increase reaction rate? What is the more important of the two contributing effects?",choices:["Collision frequency increases slightly, but the dominant effect is a large increase in the proportion of particles with energy greater than E<sub>a</sub>","Only collision frequency increases; the proportion above E<sub>a</sub> stays exactly the same","Increasing temperature only increases the activation energy","Temperature has no measurable effect on either factor"],explain:"Collision frequency rises modestly with temperature, but the proportion of particles exceeding the activation energy rises much more sharply, which is the dominant cause of the rate increase."},
{ch:17,q:"Why does increasing the concentration of reactants increase the rate of a reaction?",choices:["Particles are closer together on average, increasing the frequency of collisions between reactant particles","A higher concentration increases the activation energy required for a successful collision","A higher concentration decreases the average kinetic energy of the particles","A higher concentration only affects reactions that produce a gas"],explain:"At higher concentration, particles are closer together on average, increasing collision frequency and so the frequency of successful collisions."},
{ch:17,q:"For a reaction involving gases, why does increasing the pressure increase the rate of reaction?",choices:["Higher pressure compresses the gas, effectively increasing its concentration and so increasing collision frequency","Higher pressure increases the average kinetic energy of the gas particles at a given temperature","Higher pressure lowers the activation energy of the reaction","Higher pressure only affects reactions that are exothermic"],explain:"Compressing a gas into a smaller volume effectively increases its concentration, increasing collision frequency and so rate."},
{ch:17,q:"Marble chips are replaced with marble powder of the same total mass in a reaction with dilute hydrochloric acid. Why does the reaction proceed more vigorously with the powder?",choices:["Subdividing the solid increases its total surface area, allowing more contact and collision with the acid","Powdered marble has a different chemical formula","Powdered marble reacts with a lower activation energy","Powdered marble increases the concentration of the acid"],explain:"Subdividing a solid into smaller particles increases its total surface area without changing its mass, allowing more contact with the acid and so increasing rate."},
{ch:17,q:"Which is the correct definition of a catalyst?",choices:["A substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without itself undergoing permanent chemical change","A substance that increases the rate of a reaction by being permanently consumed","A substance that decreases the activation energy by raising the temperature","A substance that increases the yield of a reaction by shifting the position of equilibrium"],explain:"A catalyst increases reaction rate without undergoing any permanent chemical change itself."},
{ch:17,q:"How does a catalyst typically increase the rate of a reaction?",choices:["By providing an alternative reaction pathway that has a lower activation energy","By increasing the average kinetic energy of the reacting particles","By increasing the concentration of the reactants","By increasing the enthalpy change, &Delta;H, of the reaction"],explain:"Most catalysts work by providing an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy, increasing the proportion of particles able to react without any temperature change."},
{ch:17,q:"Why do catalysts have no effect on the position of equilibrium or the overall yield of a reversible reaction?",choices:["Catalysts lower the activation energy of the forward and reverse reactions by an equal amount, so equilibrium is reached faster but at the same position","Catalysts only ever affect the forward reaction, never the reverse reaction","Catalysts increase the activation energy of the reverse reaction only","Catalysts change &Delta;H of the forward reaction but not the reverse reaction"],explain:"An equal reduction in the activation energy of both forward and reverse reactions speeds both up equally, so equilibrium is reached faster without shifting its position."},
{ch:17,q:"What is the key distinction between a homogeneous and a heterogeneous catalyst?",choices:["A homogeneous catalyst is in the same phase as the reactants, while a heterogeneous catalyst is in a different phase","A homogeneous catalyst is always a solid, while a heterogeneous catalyst is always a gas","A homogeneous catalyst can only be used once, while a heterogeneous catalyst can be reused indefinitely","A homogeneous catalyst always increases the activation energy, while a heterogeneous catalyst always decreases it"],explain:"A homogeneous catalyst shares the phase of the reactants (e.g. gaseous chlorine radicals in ozone breakdown); a heterogeneous catalyst is in a different phase (e.g. solid V<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> with gaseous SO<sub>2</sub> and O<sub>2</sub>)."},
{ch:17,q:"Catalytic converters use transition metal catalysts to convert carbon monoxide and nitrogen monoxide into less harmful gases. Which equation correctly represents this?",choices:["2CO(g) + 2NO(g) &rarr; 2CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + N<sub>2</sub>(g)","CO(g) + NO(g) &rarr; CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + N(g)","2CO(g) + NO(g) &rarr; 2CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + N(g)","CO(g) + 2NO(g) &rarr; CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + N<sub>2</sub>O(g)"],explain:"2CO(g) + 2NO(g) &rarr; 2CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + N<sub>2</sub>(g) conserves carbon, oxygen and nitrogen atoms on both sides."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the general rate equation rate = k[A]<sup>m</sup>[B]<sup>n</sup>, what does the exponent m represent?",choices:["The order of reaction with respect to reactant A","The stoichiometric coefficient of A in the balanced equation","The molecularity of the overall reaction","The number of moles of A used in the experiment"],explain:"The exponent to which a reactant's concentration is raised in the rate equation is defined as the order of reaction with respect to that reactant."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: How is the overall order of a reaction calculated from its rate equation, rate = k[A]<sup>m</sup>[B]<sup>n</sup>?",choices:["It is the sum of the individual orders, m + n","It is the product of the individual orders, m &times; n","It is always equal to the number of reactants in the balanced equation","It is always equal to 2, regardless of m and n"],explain:"The overall order of reaction is defined as the sum of the orders with respect to each individual reactant, m + n."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: The reaction 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2NO(g) &rarr; 2H<sub>2</sub>O(g) + N<sub>2</sub>(g) is second order with respect to NO and first order with respect to H<sub>2</sub>. What is the rate equation and overall order?",choices:["rate = k[NO]<sup>2</sup>[H<sub>2</sub>]; third order","rate = k[NO][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>; third order","rate = k[NO]<sup>2</sup>[H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>; fourth order","rate = k[NO][H<sub>2</sub>]; second order"],explain:"Second order wrt NO means [NO]<sup>2</sup>; first order wrt H<sub>2</sub> means [H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>1</sup>. rate = k[NO]<sup>2</sup>[H<sub>2</sub>], overall order = 2 + 1 = 3."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the reaction 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2NO(g) &rarr; 2H<sub>2</sub>O(g) + N<sub>2</sub>(g), the experimental orders (2 for NO, 1 for H<sub>2</sub>) do not match their coefficients in the balanced equation (both 2). What does this illustrate?",choices:["Orders of reaction cannot be predicted from stoichiometric coefficients; they must be found experimentally","The balanced equation for this reaction must be incorrect","Orders of reaction are always equal to half the stoichiometric coefficient","This can only happen for reactions involving a catalyst"],explain:"Reaction orders bear no necessary relationship to the coefficients of the overall balanced equation; they can only be determined empirically."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2HI(g), the reaction is found to be first order with respect to both H<sub>2</sub> and I<sub>2</sub>. What is the overall order?",choices:["Second order","First order","Third order","Zero order"],explain:"Overall order = order wrt H<sub>2</sub> + order wrt I<sub>2</sub> = 1 + 1 = 2, second order overall."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: The decomposition 2H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>(aq) &rarr; 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) is first order with respect to H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, the only species in the rate equation. What is the overall order of reaction?",choices:["First order","Second order, matching the coefficient of 2 in the equation","Zero order, since O<sub>2</sub> is a product","Cannot be determined without further data"],explain:"With only one species in the rate equation, raised to the power 1, the overall order equals the order with respect to that one reactant: first order."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>8</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + 2I<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; 2SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + I<sub>2</sub>(aq), the reaction is found to be first order with respect to both S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>8</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> and I<sup>&minus;</sup>. What is the rate equation and overall order?",choices:["rate = k[S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>8</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>][I<sup>&minus;</sup>]; second order","rate = k[S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>8</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>][I<sup>&minus;</sup>]<sup>2</sup>; third order","rate = k[S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>8</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>]<sup>2</sup>[I<sup>&minus;</sup>]<sup>2</sup>; fourth order","rate = k[I<sup>&minus;</sup>]; first order"],explain:"Both species are first order, giving rate = k[S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>8</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>][I<sup>&minus;</sup>], overall order 1 + 1 = 2, even though the equation shows a coefficient of 2 for I<sup>&minus;</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Which method is used at this level to determine the order of reaction with respect to each reactant from experimental data?",choices:["The initial rates method, comparing experiments with different starting concentrations while others are held constant","Measuring the final equilibrium concentration of each reactant","Measuring the overall enthalpy change of the reaction","Counting the number of atoms in the balanced equation"],explain:"The initial rates method varies the starting concentration of one reactant at a time, holding others constant, and compares the resulting initial rates to deduce each order."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the initial rates method, if doubling the concentration of reactant A has no effect on the rate, what is the order of reaction with respect to A?",choices:["Zero order","First order","Second order","Third order"],explain:"If changing [A] produces no change in rate, the reaction does not depend on [A] at all, so it is zero order with respect to A."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the initial rates method, if tripling the concentration of reactant A triples the rate, what is the order of reaction with respect to A?",choices:["First order","Zero order","Second order","Third order"],explain:"A directly proportional change between concentration and rate (tripling [A] triples the rate) indicates first order with respect to A."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the initial rates method, if doubling the concentration of reactant A causes the rate to increase four-fold, what is the order of reaction with respect to A?",choices:["Second order","First order","Zero order","Fourth order"],explain:"A four-fold (2<sup>2</sup>) increase in rate when [A] doubles indicates that rate &prop; [A]<sup>2</sup>, i.e. second order with respect to A."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Using the data: Exp 1, [A] = 0.10, [B] = 0.10, rate = 2.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>; Exp 2, [A] = 0.20, [B] = 0.10, rate = 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>; Exp 3, [A] = 0.30, [B] = 0.10, rate = 6.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What is the order of reaction with respect to A?",choices:["First order","Zero order","Second order","Cannot be determined from this data"],explain:"With [B] held constant, doubling [A] (Exp 1 to 2) doubles the rate, and tripling [A] (Exp 1 to 3) triples the rate &ndash; a directly proportional relationship, so first order with respect to A."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Continuing the same data set: Exp 3, [A] = 0.30, [B] = 0.10, rate = 6.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>; Exp 4, [A] = 0.30, [B] = 0.20, rate = 2.4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>; Exp 5, [A] = 0.30, [B] = 0.30, rate = 5.4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What is the order of reaction with respect to B?",choices:["Second order","First order","Zero order","Third order"],explain:"With [A] held constant, doubling [B] (Exp 3 to 4) quadruples the rate (6.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> to 2.4 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>, a factor of 4 = 2<sup>2</sup>), and tripling [B] (Exp 3 to 5) gives a nine-fold increase (factor of 9 = 3<sup>2</sup>), so second order with respect to B."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Combining the results that the reaction is first order with respect to A and second order with respect to B, what is the overall rate equation and overall order?",choices:["rate = k[A][B]<sup>2</sup>; third order overall","rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup>[B]; third order overall","rate = k[A][B]; second order overall","rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup>[B]<sup>2</sup>; fourth order overall"],explain:"Combining first order in A with second order in B gives rate = k[A][B]<sup>2</sup>, with overall order 1 + 2 = 3."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For the reaction between NO<sub>2</sub> and F<sub>2</sub>: Exp 1, [NO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.1, [F<sub>2</sub>] = 0.2, rate = 0.1; Exp 2, [NO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.2, [F<sub>2</sub>] = 0.2, rate = 0.4 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> min<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What does comparing experiments 1 and 2 show about the order with respect to NO<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["Second order, since doubling [NO<sub>2</sub>] (with [F<sub>2</sub>] constant) quadruples the rate","First order, since doubling [NO<sub>2</sub>] doubles the rate","Zero order, since the rate does not depend on [NO<sub>2</sub>]","Third order, since the rate increases eight-fold"],explain:"With [F<sub>2</sub>] held constant, doubling [NO<sub>2</sub>] from 0.1 to 0.2 increases the rate from 0.1 to 0.4, a four-fold (2<sup>2</sup>) increase, indicating second order with respect to NO<sub>2</sub>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Continuing the NO<sub>2</sub> + F<sub>2</sub> data: Exp 1, [NO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.1, [F<sub>2</sub>] = 0.2, rate = 0.1; Exp 3, [NO<sub>2</sub>] = 0.1, [F<sub>2</sub>] = 0.4, rate = 0.2 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> min<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What is the order of reaction with respect to F<sub>2</sub>, and what is the full rate equation?",choices:["First order with respect to F<sub>2</sub>; rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>[F<sub>2</sub>]","Second order with respect to F<sub>2</sub>; rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>[F<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>","Zero order with respect to F<sub>2</sub>; rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>","First order with respect to F<sub>2</sub>; rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>][F<sub>2</sub>]"],explain:"With [NO<sub>2</sub>] held constant, doubling [F<sub>2</sub>] doubles the rate (0.1 to 0.2), so F<sub>2</sub> is first order. Combined with second order in NO<sub>2</sub>, the rate equation is rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>[F<sub>2</sub>]."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g): Exp 1, [NO] = 0.30, [O<sub>2</sub>] = 0.20, rate = 2.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>; Exp 2, [NO] = 0.30, [O<sub>2</sub>] = 0.40, rate = 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What does this show about the order with respect to O<sub>2</sub>?",choices:["First order, since doubling [O<sub>2</sub>] (with [NO] constant) doubles the rate","Second order, since doubling [O<sub>2</sub>] quadruples the rate","Zero order, since the rate is unaffected by [O<sub>2</sub>]","Cannot be determined from only two experiments"],explain:"With [NO] held constant, doubling [O<sub>2</sub>] from 0.20 to 0.40 doubles the rate from 2.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> to 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>, indicating first order with respect to O<sub>2</sub>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Continuing the NO + O<sub>2</sub> data: Exp 2, [NO] = 0.30, [O<sub>2</sub>] = 0.40, rate = 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>; Exp 3, [NO] = 0.60, [O<sub>2</sub>] = 0.80, rate = 3.2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Given that the reaction is first order with respect to O<sub>2</sub>, what is the order with respect to NO?",choices:["Second order","First order","Zero order","Third order"],explain:"From Exp 2 to 3, both [NO] and [O<sub>2</sub>] double, and the rate increases eight-fold (4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> to 3.2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup>). Since O<sub>2</sub> alone contributes a factor of 2, NO must contribute a factor of 4 (= 2<sup>2</sup>), so NO is second order."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g), with NO second order and O<sub>2</sub> first order, calculate the rate constant, k, using Exp 1 data: [NO] = 0.30 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, [O<sub>2</sub>] = 0.20 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, rate = 2.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>.",choices:["0.111 mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","2.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","6.67 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","33.3 mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"rate = k[NO]<sup>2</sup>[O<sub>2</sub>], so k = 2.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> / (0.30<sup>2</sup> &times; 0.20) = 2.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> / 0.018 = 0.111 mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>, with units consistent with a third-order reaction."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: The decomposition 2N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>(g) &rarr; 4NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) has a measured rate constant with units of s<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What does this tell you about the overall order of the reaction, and the likely rate equation?",choices:["The reaction is first order overall, with rate equation rate = k[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>]","The reaction is second order overall, with rate equation rate = k[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>]<sup>2</sup>","The reaction is zero order overall, with rate equation rate = k","The reaction is third order overall, matching the sum of the coefficients"],explain:"Units of s<sup>&minus;1</sup> for k are characteristic of a first-order reaction, consistent with rate = k[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>], even though the coefficient of N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> in the balanced equation is 2."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On a concentration&ndash;time graph for a zero-order reaction, what shape is the curve, and what does its gradient represent?",choices:["A straight line with constant negative gradient, equal in magnitude to the rate constant, k","An exponential decay curve, with gradient decreasing over time","A curve that is initially flat and then falls steeply","A horizontal straight line, since concentration never changes"],explain:"For a zero-order reaction, rate = k is constant, so concentration falls at a constant rate, giving a straight-line concentration&ndash;time graph with gradient of magnitude k."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On a rate&ndash;concentration graph for a zero-order reaction, what is the shape of the graph?",choices:["A horizontal straight line, since rate does not depend on concentration","A straight line through the origin with positive gradient","A curve rising steeply at low concentration and levelling off at high concentration","A parabola, characteristic of a squared relationship"],explain:"Since rate = k for a zero-order reaction, rate is independent of concentration, giving a horizontal straight line on a rate&ndash;concentration graph."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On a concentration&ndash;time graph for a first-order reaction, what shape is the curve, and what key feature distinguishes it?",choices:["An exponential decay curve with a constant half-life, independent of how far the reaction has progressed","A straight line with constant gradient","A curve identical in every respect to a second-order concentration&ndash;time curve","A horizontal straight line"],explain:"A first-order reaction gives an exponential decay concentration&ndash;time curve, with the defining feature that its half-life, t<sub>&frac12;</sub>, is constant throughout the reaction."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On a rate&ndash;concentration graph for a first-order reaction, what is the shape of the graph, and what does its gradient equal?",choices:["A straight line through the origin, with gradient equal to the rate constant, k","A horizontal straight line, with height equal to k","A parabola passing through the origin","A curve that approaches, but never reaches, the x-axis"],explain:"Since rate = k[A] for a first-order reaction, rate is directly proportional to [A], giving a straight line through the origin with gradient k."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Iodine-131 has a half-life, t<sub>&frac12;</sub>, of 8 days. Starting from a sample of 0.680 g, calculate the mass of <sup>131</sup>I remaining after 24 days.",choices:["0.0850 g","0.340 g","0.170 g","0.0425 g"],explain:"24 days = 3 half-lives (24 &divide; 8). Mass remaining = 0.680 &times; (&frac12;)<sup>3</sup> = 0.680 &divide; 8 = 0.0850 g, since radioactive decay is a first-order process with a constant half-life."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On a rate&ndash;concentration graph for a second-order reaction, what is the characteristic shape of the graph?",choices:["A parabola, characteristic of the square function, with gradient initially zero and increasing with concentration","A straight line through the origin","A horizontal straight line","An exponential decay curve"],explain:"Since rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup> for a second-order reaction, a rate&ndash;concentration graph is a parabola, the signature shape of a squared relationship."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why do concentration&ndash;time graphs alone not clearly distinguish a first-order reaction from a second-order reaction, even though their rate&ndash;concentration graphs look very different?",choices:["Both give a curve that decreases and levels off over time, making the two shapes difficult to distinguish by eye alone","First-order concentration&ndash;time graphs are always straight lines, which is clearly different from second order","Second-order reactions never show any change in concentration over time","Concentration&ndash;time graphs can only be plotted for zero-order reactions"],explain:"Both first- and second-order reactions give a concentration&ndash;time curve that falls and levels off, making them hard to distinguish visually; a rate&ndash;concentration graph (straight line versus parabola) gives a much clearer distinction."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: How does the half-life of a zero-order reaction change as the reaction proceeds, in contrast to a first-order reaction?",choices:["It gets progressively shorter, since the same constant rate acts on an ever-smaller amount of remaining reactant","It gets progressively longer, for the same reason as a second-order reaction","It stays constant, exactly like a first-order reaction","It cannot be defined for a zero-order reaction"],explain:"For a zero-order reaction, rate is constant, so as concentration falls, each successive halving of concentration takes less time than the last, meaning the half-life decreases."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: How does the half-life of a second-order reaction change as the reaction proceeds?",choices:["It gets progressively longer, since the rate itself falls as concentration falls, taking disproportionately longer to halve a smaller amount","It gets progressively shorter, exactly like a zero-order reaction","It stays constant, exactly like a first-order reaction","Second-order reactions never reach a measurable half-life"],explain:"For a second-order reaction, rate falls sharply as concentration falls (rate &prop; [A]<sup>2</sup>), so each successive half-life takes longer than the one before."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What are the units of the rate constant, k, for a zero-order reaction (with time measured in seconds)?",choices:["mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"For a zero-order reaction, rate = k, so k has the same units as rate itself: mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What are the units of the rate constant, k, for a first-order reaction (with time measured in seconds)?",choices:["s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"For rate = k[A], k = rate / [A] = (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>) / (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>) = s<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What are the units of the rate constant, k, for a second-order reaction (with time measured in seconds)?",choices:["mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"For rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup>, k = rate / [A]<sup>2</sup> = (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>) / (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What are the units of the rate constant, k, for a third-order reaction (with time measured in seconds)?",choices:["mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"For rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup>[B] (third order overall), k = rate / ([A]<sup>2</sup>[B]) = (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>) / (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: A reaction has rate equation rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup>[B]. When [A] = [B] = 2.50 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>, the rate is 7.75 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> min<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Calculate the value of k.",choices:["4.96 mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> min<sup>&minus;1</sup>","7.75 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> min<sup>&minus;1</sup>","0.124 mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> min<sup>&minus;1</sup>","1.95 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> min<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"k = rate / ([A]<sup>2</sup>[B]) = 7.75 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> / (2.50 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = 7.75 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> / 1.5625 &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> = 4.96 mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> min<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why is the catalytic decomposition of ammonia on heated platinum, 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g) &rarr; N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g), zero order overall?",choices:["The rate depends only on the small, fixed number of NH<sub>3</sub> molecules attached to the saturated catalyst surface, not on the bulk gas concentration","Ammonia does not actually decompose under these conditions","The reaction has no measurable rate constant","Platinum acts as a reactant, not a catalyst, in this reaction"],explain:"With the catalyst surface fully saturated with NH<sub>3</sub>, the rate depends only on the fixed, limited surface area available, so increasing the bulk gas-phase concentration of NH<sub>3</sub> has no further effect on rate."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: A primary halogenoalkane reacts with aqueous hydroxide ions via an S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism, giving rate = k[halogenoalkane][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]. What are the overall order of this reaction and the units of k?",choices:["Second order; mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","First order; s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Third order; mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Zero order; mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"rate = k[halogenoalkane][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>] is second order overall (1 + 1), giving k units of mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: A tertiary halogenoalkane reacts with aqueous hydroxide ions via an S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism, giving rate = k[halogenoalkane]. What are the overall order of this reaction and the units of k?",choices:["First order; s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Second order; mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Zero order; mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Third order; mol<sup>&minus;2</sup> dm<sup>6</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"rate = k[halogenoalkane] is first order overall, giving k units of s<sup>&minus;1</sup> &ndash; notably independent of [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What is an 'elementary step' in the context of a reaction mechanism?",choices:["A single, simple molecular event that forms part of a multi-step sequence leading from reactants to products","The overall balanced equation for the reaction","Any step in a mechanism that can be directly observed experimentally","The step that uses the fewest reactant particles, regardless of its rate"],explain:"An elementary step is a single, simple molecular event; most reactions proceed as a sequence of such steps, collectively called the reaction mechanism."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What does the term 'molecularity' describe for an elementary step?",choices:["The number of reactant particles taking part in that specific step","The overall order of the reaction as a whole","The number of different elements present in the step","The number of steps in the overall mechanism"],explain:"Molecularity refers to the number of reactant particles involved in a single elementary step: unimolecular (one), bimolecular (two), or termolecular (three)."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why are termolecular elementary steps (involving three reactant particles) rare in reaction mechanisms?",choices:["The simultaneous collision of three particles with sufficient energy and correct orientation is statistically very improbable","Three-particle collisions are forbidden by the law of conservation of mass","Termolecular steps can never be bimolecular steps in disguise","Termolecular steps only occur in reactions involving a catalyst"],explain:"Requiring three particles to collide simultaneously, with sufficient energy and correct orientation all at once, is far less statistically likely than a two-particle collision, so mechanisms generally favour unimolecular and bimolecular steps."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Which is the correct definition of the rate-determining step (RDS) in a reaction mechanism?",choices:["The slowest elementary step, which has the highest activation energy and determines the rate equation for the overall reaction","The step that occurs last in the sequence of elementary steps","The fastest elementary step in the mechanism","The step in which the catalyst, if present, is regenerated"],explain:"The rate-determining step is the slowest step of the mechanism, equivalent to the step with the highest activation energy; products can only form as fast as this step allows, so it fixes the overall rate equation."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For the elementary step 2A &rarr; products, what is its molecularity and rate equation?",choices:["Bimolecular; rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup>","Unimolecular; rate = k[A]","Termolecular; rate = k[A]<sup>3</sup>","Bimolecular; rate = k[A]"],explain:"2A &rarr; products involves two molecules of A colliding, a bimolecular step, giving rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup> directly from its molecularity."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For the elementary step A + B &rarr; products, what is its molecularity and rate equation?",choices:["Bimolecular; rate = k[A][B]","Unimolecular; rate = k[A]","Termolecular; rate = k[A][B]<sup>2</sup>","Bimolecular; rate = k[A]<sup>2</sup>[B]"],explain:"A + B &rarr; products is bimolecular (two reactant particles, A and B), giving rate = k[A][B] directly from its molecularity."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For 2NO<sub>2</sub>Cl(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g), with mechanism step 1 (slow, RDS) NO<sub>2</sub>Cl(g) &rarr; NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + Cl(g), step 2 (fast) NO<sub>2</sub>Cl(g) + Cl(g) &rarr; NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g), what is the rate equation for the overall reaction?",choices:["rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>Cl]; first order","rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>Cl]<sup>2</sup>; second order, matching the balanced equation","rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>Cl][Cl]; second order","rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>][Cl<sub>2</sub>]; second order"],explain:"Since step 1 is both the first step and the rate-determining step, the overall rate equation is simply its rate equation: rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>Cl], first order &ndash; even though the balanced equation has a coefficient of 2 for NO<sub>2</sub>Cl."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g), with mechanism step 1 (fast) NO(g) + NO(g) &rarr; N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>(g), step 2 (slow, RDS) N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g), why can the rate equation not simply be written as rate = k[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>][O<sub>2</sub>]?",choices:["Because N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> is an intermediate, not a reactant in the overall equation, so its concentration must be substituted using the fast pre-equilibrium step","Because intermediates always have a concentration of exactly zero","Because the rate-determining step must always be written using only the overall reactants","Because N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> does not actually form during this reaction"],explain:"N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> is produced and consumed within the mechanism (an intermediate), so it cannot appear in the final rate equation; since the fast first step shows [N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>] depends on [NO]<sup>2</sup>, this is substituted in to eliminate the intermediate."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Continuing the 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) mechanism, after eliminating the intermediate N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> using [N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>] &prop; [NO]<sup>2</sup>, what is the final rate equation and overall order?",choices:["rate = k[NO]<sup>2</sup>[O<sub>2</sub>]; third order","rate = k[NO][O<sub>2</sub>]; second order","rate = k[NO]<sup>2</sup>[O<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>; fourth order","rate = k[O<sub>2</sub>]; first order"],explain:"Substituting [N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>] &prop; [NO]<sup>2</sup> into rate = k[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>][O<sub>2</sub>] gives rate = k[NO]<sup>2</sup>[O<sub>2</sub>], a third-order reaction overall, matching the experimentally observed kinetics of this real reaction."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the acid-catalyzed iodination of propanone, CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>(aq) + I<sub>2</sub>(aq) &rarr; CH<sub>2</sub>ICOCH<sub>3</sub>(aq) + HI(aq), the experimental rate equation is rate = k[CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>][H<sup>+</sup>], zero order with respect to I<sub>2</sub>. What does this reveal about the mechanism?",choices:["The rate-determining step involves propanone and the H<sup>+</sup> catalyst, but not I<sub>2</sub>, which only reacts in a later, fast step","I<sub>2</sub> must be a catalyst rather than a reactant in this mechanism","The reaction has no rate-determining step at all","H<sup>+</sup> cannot be involved in the mechanism, since it is a catalyst"],explain:"Since I<sub>2</sub> does not appear in the rate equation, it plays no part in the rate-determining step; the slow step is the acid-catalyzed enolization of propanone, with I<sub>2</sub> only reacting with the enol in a subsequent fast step."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the propanone iodination example above, H<sup>+</sup> is a catalyst, yet it appears in the experimental rate equation, rate = k[CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>][H<sup>+</sup>]. How is this possible, given that H<sup>+</sup> does not appear in the overall balanced equation?",choices:["A catalyst can appear in the rate equation if it takes part in the rate-determining step; it is simply regenerated later in the mechanism, so it cancels out of the overall equation","This is an error; catalysts can never appear in a rate equation","H<sup>+</sup> must actually be a reactant, not a catalyst, in this reaction","The rate equation must be rate = k[CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>][I<sub>2</sub>] instead"],explain:"A catalyst consumed in the rate-determining step (and regenerated in a later step) will appear in the experimental rate equation even though it cancels out of, and so is absent from, the overall balanced equation."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl(aq) + OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>COH(aq) + Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), with mechanism step 1 (slow, RDS) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C<sup>+</sup> + Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>, step 2 (fast) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C<sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>COH, what is the rate equation?",choices:["rate = k[(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl]; independent of [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]","rate = k[(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]; second order overall","rate = k[OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]; independent of [(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl]","rate = k[(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>&minus;</sup>]; second order overall"],explain:"Since the RDS is the unimolecular ionization of (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl alone, the rate equation is rate = k[(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl], first order overall and independent of [OH<sup>&minus;</sup>], even though OH<sup>&minus;</sup> appears in the overall balanced equation."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why is the above (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl + OH<sup>&minus;</sup> mechanism a particularly important illustration of a key HL concept in kinetics?",choices:["It shows that a reactant can appear in the overall balanced equation yet be completely absent from the rate equation, because it plays no part in the rate-determining step","It shows that all nucleophilic substitution reactions must be first order","It shows that OH<sup>&minus;</sup> can never act as a nucleophile","It shows that rate equations can never be determined for substitution reactions"],explain:"This S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism demonstrates that a species required by the overall stoichiometry (OH<sup>&minus;</sup>) need not appear in the rate equation at all, if it is not involved in the (here, unimolecular) rate-determining step."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What is the key distinction between a reaction intermediate and a transition state?",choices:["An intermediate exists for a finite time and could in principle be isolated, while a transition state exists only fleetingly, as bonds are breaking and forming, and cannot be isolated","An intermediate can never be detected, while a transition state can always be isolated","An intermediate and a transition state are simply two different names for the same species","A transition state is always a product of the reaction, while an intermediate never is"],explain:"An intermediate is a genuine, if short-lived, chemical species that exists for a finite time between steps; a transition state is a fleeting, high-energy arrangement that exists only during bond-breaking and bond-forming, and can never be isolated."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On an energy profile for a two-step reaction, what do the two maxima (peaks) represent, and what does the dip between them represent?",choices:["The two maxima are the transition states of each step; the dip between them is the intermediate","The two maxima are the two reactants; the dip is the product","The two maxima are both intermediates; the dip is a transition state","The maxima represent catalysts; the dip represents the activation energy"],explain:"Each peak on a multistep energy profile corresponds to a transition state (one per elementary step); the local minimum between consecutive peaks corresponds to the intermediate species formed after the first step."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On an energy profile for a two-step reaction where the first peak is higher than the second, which step is rate-determining, and what is the activation energy of the overall reaction?",choices:["The first step is rate-determining, and the overall activation energy equals that of the first step","The second step is rate-determining, and the overall activation energy equals that of the second step","Both steps are equally rate-determining","The overall activation energy is the sum of both individual activation energies"],explain:"The step with the higher activation energy is the slower step and so the rate-determining step; the activation energy for the overall reaction is taken as that of this rate-determining step."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: If a reactant does not appear at all in the experimentally determined rate equation (zero order with respect to it), what does this tell you about its role in the mechanism?",choices:["It plays no part in the rate-determining step, even though it is still required overall for the reaction to occur","The reactant is not actually involved in the reaction at all","The reactant must be a catalyst rather than a true reactant","The reaction mechanism must consist of only one step"],explain:"A reactant that does not appear in the rate equation is not involved in the (rate-limiting) slowest step of the mechanism, even though it is still consumed overall, reacting only in a step that occurs after the rate-determining step."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why can a proposed reaction mechanism never be definitively 'proven' correct, even if it is fully consistent with the kinetic and stoichiometric data?",choices:["Because kinetic evidence can only show that a mechanism is consistent with the data, not that it is the unique correct explanation; an alternative mechanism might fit equally well","Because reaction mechanisms are purely a matter of opinion with no supporting evidence","Because kinetic data can never be collected accurately enough to test any mechanism","Because all proposed mechanisms are eventually shown to be incorrect"],explain:"A mechanism consistent with the available evidence is described as a 'possible mechanism'; it cannot be proven uniquely correct, since other mechanisms might also be consistent with the same data."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What does the Arrhenius equation, k = Ae<sup>&minus;E<sub>a</sub>/RT</sup>, directly describe?",choices:["The temperature dependence of the rate constant, k, in terms of the activation energy, E<sub>a</sub>","The dependence of equilibrium position on temperature","The relationship between bond enthalpy and reaction rate","The dependence of reaction order on temperature"],explain:"The Arrhenius equation expresses how the rate constant, k, depends on absolute temperature, T, and activation energy, E<sub>a</sub>, with A and R as constants."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the Arrhenius equation, k = Ae<sup>&minus;E<sub>a</sub>/RT</sup>, what does the term A represent?",choices:["The Arrhenius factor (pre-exponential or frequency factor), which accounts for the frequency of collisions with correct orientation","The activation energy of the reaction","The gas constant","The overall order of the reaction"],explain:"A, the Arrhenius factor, accounts for the frequency of collisions with the correct geometry; it is a constant for a given reaction, with the same units as k."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why must the effect of increasing temperature on reaction rate act entirely through the rate constant, k, rather than through reactant concentrations?",choices:["Increasing temperature does not change the concentrations of reactants, so any change in rate must come from a change in k","Increasing temperature always increases reactant concentrations proportionally","The rate equation itself changes form at different temperatures","Reactant concentrations are not part of the rate equation"],explain:"Since temperature has no direct effect on the numerical value of reactant concentrations, and rate = k[A]<sup>m</sup>[B]<sup>n</sup>, any temperature-dependence of rate must arise from a temperature-dependence of k."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: What is the linear (logarithmic) form of the Arrhenius equation?",choices:["ln k = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T) + ln A","ln k = E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; T + ln A","k = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T) + A","ln k = &minus;E<sub>a</sub> &times; R &times; T + ln A"],explain:"Taking the natural log of both sides of k = Ae<sup>&minus;E<sub>a</sub>/RT</sup> gives ln k = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T) + ln A, the equation of a straight line with y = ln k and x = 1/T."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On an Arrhenius plot of ln k (y-axis) against 1/T (x-axis), what does the gradient of the line equal?",choices:["&minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R","E<sub>a</sub>/R","ln A","A/R"],explain:"Comparing ln k = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T) + ln A with y = mx + c shows that the gradient, m, equals &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: On an Arrhenius plot of ln k against 1/T, what does the y-intercept of the line equal?",choices:["ln A","&minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R","A","E<sub>a</sub>"],explain:"Comparing ln k = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T) + ln A with y = mx + c shows that the y-intercept, c, equals ln A."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why does an Arrhenius plot of ln k against 1/T always have a negative gradient?",choices:["Because E<sub>a</sub> and R are both positive quantities, making &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R negative","Because k always decreases as temperature increases","Because 1/T is always a negative quantity","Because ln k is always a negative quantity"],explain:"Since both the activation energy, E<sub>a</sub>, and the gas constant, R, are positive, the gradient &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R is always negative, meaning ln k decreases as 1/T increases (i.e. as T decreases) &ndash; consistent with k increasing as temperature rises."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: An Arrhenius plot gives a measured gradient of &minus;9518.65 K. Using R = 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, calculate the activation energy in kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>.",choices:["79.1 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","9518.65 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","0.0791 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","1146 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"E<sub>a</sub> = &minus;(gradient) &times; R = 9518.65 K &times; 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> = 79 100 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, which converts to 79.1 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> on dividing by 1000."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why must a calculated E<sub>a</sub> value in J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> be divided by 1000 to express it in kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>?",choices:["Because R is given in J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, so multiplying by the gradient (in K) gives E<sub>a</sub> directly in J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, and 1 kJ = 1000 J","Because E<sub>a</sub> is always reported to three decimal places","Because the gas constant, R, must first be converted into kJ K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Because the Arrhenius equation only works with energies in kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"Since R = 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, multiplying by a gradient in kelvin gives E<sub>a</sub> directly in J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>; dividing by 1000 converts this into the more commonly quoted kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For a first-order reaction, an Arrhenius plot gave a y-intercept of &minus;1.20. Calculate the Arrhenius factor, A, including units.",choices:["0.301 s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;1.20 s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","1.20 s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","e<sup>1.20</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"Since the y-intercept = ln A, A = e<sup>(intercept)</sup> = e<sup>&minus;1.20</sup> = 0.301 s<sup>&minus;1</sup>; A takes the same units as k, here s<sup>&minus;1</sup> for a first-order reaction."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For a second-order reaction (N<sub>2</sub>O decomposition), an Arrhenius plot gave a y-intercept of 28.1. Calculate the Arrhenius factor, A, including units.",choices:["1.60 &times; 10<sup>12</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","28.1 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","e<sup>&minus;28.1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","2.81 &times; 10<sup>1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"A = e<sup>(intercept)</sup> = e<sup>28.1</sup> &asymp; 1.60 &times; 10<sup>12</sup>, with units matching k for a second-order reaction, mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Which formula correctly relates the rate constants k<sub>1</sub> and k<sub>2</sub> at two different temperatures T<sub>1</sub> and T<sub>2</sub> to the activation energy, E<sub>a</sub>?",choices:["ln(k<sub>2</sub>/k<sub>1</sub>) = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>)","ln(k<sub>2</sub>/k<sub>1</sub>) = E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (T<sub>2</sub> &minus; T<sub>1</sub>)","k<sub>2</sub>/k<sub>1</sub> = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>)","ln(k<sub>2</sub> &minus; k<sub>1</sub>) = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>)"],explain:"Subtracting the linear Arrhenius equation at T<sub>1</sub> from that at T<sub>2</sub> eliminates ln A, giving ln(k<sub>2</sub>/k<sub>1</sub>) = &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R &times; (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>), which allows E<sub>a</sub> to be found from just two (k, T) data pairs."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For 2HI(g) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g), k<sub>1</sub> = 3.52 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 283&deg;C (556 K) and k<sub>2</sub> = 3.95 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 508&deg;C (781 K). What is the value of ln(k<sub>2</sub>/k<sub>1</sub>)?",choices:["11.63","1.122 &times; 10<sup>5</sup>","&minus;11.63","5.182 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>"],explain:"k<sub>2</sub>/k<sub>1</sub> = 3.95 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> / 3.52 &times; 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> = 1.122 &times; 10<sup>5</sup>; taking the natural log gives ln(1.122 &times; 10<sup>5</sup>) = 11.63."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Continuing the HI decomposition calculation, with T<sub>1</sub> = 556 K and T<sub>2</sub> = 781 K, what is the value of (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>)?",choices:["&minus;5.182 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> K<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+5.182 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> K<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;2.25 &times; 10<sup>&minus;1</sup> K<sup>&minus;1</sup>","225 K<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub> = 1/781 &minus; 1/556 = 1.280 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> &minus; 1.799 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> = &minus;5.182 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> K<sup>&minus;1</sup>, negative because T<sub>2</sub> &gt; T<sub>1</sub>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Using ln(k<sub>2</sub>/k<sub>1</sub>) = 11.63 and (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>) = &minus;5.182 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> K<sup>&minus;1</sup>, with R = 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, calculate E<sub>a</sub> for the decomposition of HI, in kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>.",choices:["187 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","96.6 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","11.63 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;187 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"E<sub>a</sub> = &minus;R &times; ln(k<sub>2</sub>/k<sub>1</sub>) / (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>) = &minus;8.31 &times; 11.63 / (&minus;5.182 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>) = 1.865 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; 187 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the two-point Arrhenius calculation, why must (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>) come out negative whenever T<sub>2</sub> &gt; T<sub>1</sub>, and why does this matter for the sign of the final answer?",choices:["Because 1/T<sub>2</sub> is smaller than 1/T<sub>1</sub> when T<sub>2</sub> &gt; T<sub>1</sub>; this negative value cancels the leading minus sign in &minus;E<sub>a</sub>/R, correctly giving a positive E<sub>a</sub> consistent with k<sub>2</sub> &gt; k<sub>1</sub>","Because temperatures in kelvin are always written as negative numbers in this formula","Because E<sub>a</sub> is always reported as a negative quantity in this method","It does not matter; the sign has no effect on the final numerical answer for E<sub>a</sub>"],explain:"As T increases, 1/T decreases, so (1/T<sub>2</sub> &minus; 1/T<sub>1</sub>) is negative whenever T<sub>2</sub> &gt; T<sub>1</sub>; this negative value cancels the equation's leading minus sign, yielding a positive E<sub>a</sub>, consistent with the physically required result that k<sub>2</sub> &gt; k<sub>1</sub> at the higher temperature T<sub>2</sub>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For the reaction NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + CO(g) &rarr; NO(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g), k = 1.30 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 700 K and k = 23.0 mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 800 K. Calculate the activation energy, in kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, using R = 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>.",choices:["134 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","23.9 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","2.87 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","0.134 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"ln(23.0/1.30) = 2.873; (1/800 &minus; 1/700) = &minus;1.786 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> K<sup>&minus;1</sup>. E<sub>a</sub> = &minus;8.31 &times; 2.873 / (&minus;1.786 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup>) = 1.337 &times; 10<sup>5</sup> J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; 134 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: The rate equation for the second-order decomposition of N<sub>2</sub>O is rate = k[N<sub>2</sub>O]<sup>2</sup>, with k = 0.244 dm<sup>3</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 750&deg;C. A sample starts at [N<sub>2</sub>O] = 0.200 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>. Calculate the rate once 10% of the N<sub>2</sub>O has reacted.",choices:["7.91 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","9.76 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","4.88 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","2.44 &times; 10<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"After 10% reacts, [N<sub>2</sub>O] = 0.200 &times; 0.90 = 0.180 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>. rate = k[N<sub>2</sub>O]<sup>2</sup> = 0.244 &times; (0.180)<sup>2</sup> = 0.244 &times; 0.0324 = 7.91 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why is the common 'rate doubles for every 10&deg;C rise' rule described as only a rule of thumb rather than a law?",choices:["Because the size of the temperature effect on k depends on the actual activation energy, and the rule only holds for E<sub>a</sub> in a fairly narrow range around 50 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Because temperature never actually affects the rate of any reaction","Because the rule only applies to reactions involving a catalyst","Because the rule is only valid for zero-order reactions"],explain:"The Arrhenius equation shows that the effect of a given temperature rise on k depends on E<sub>a</sub>; the doubling-every-10&deg;C pattern holds only for reactions with E<sub>a</sub> around 50 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, typical of many common reactions, but not for reactions with unusually high or low activation energies."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: A reaction has an unusually high activation energy compared to typical reactions. How would a 10&deg;C temperature rise affect its rate constant compared with the 'doubling' rule of thumb?",choices:["It would increase the rate constant by more than the usual factor of two, since a high E<sub>a</sub> makes the reaction more sensitive to temperature change","It would have no effect at all on the rate constant","It would decrease the rate constant, since high-E<sub>a</sub> reactions slow down as temperature rises","It would increase the rate constant by exactly the same factor of two, regardless of E<sub>a</sub>"],explain:"Because the exponential term in the Arrhenius equation involves E<sub>a</sub>/RT, a larger E<sub>a</sub> makes k more sensitive to a given change in T, so a reaction with unusually high E<sub>a</sub> shows a greater-than-usual increase in rate for the same temperature rise."},
{ch:17,q:"What is temperature in kelvin proportional to, according to the kinetic molecular theory, and what shape best describes the Maxwell&ndash;Boltzmann energy distribution curve?",choices:["Average kinetic energy of the particles; an asymmetric curve rising steeply then tailing off gradually at high energy","Average potential energy of the particles; a symmetric bell-shaped curve","Total mass of the particles; a straight horizontal line","Total number of collisions; a vertical line at the activation energy"],explain:"Temperature in kelvin is proportional to average kinetic energy, and the Maxwell&ndash;Boltzmann distribution is asymmetric: it rises steeply to a peak at low energy, then tails off gradually without reaching zero at high energy."},
{ch:17,q:"Which of the following correctly identifies all five main factors known to affect the rate of a chemical reaction?",choices:["Temperature, concentration, pressure (for gases), surface area, and the presence of a catalyst","Temperature, colour, mass, volume, and molar mass","Concentration, pH, boiling point, density, and viscosity","Pressure, temperature, enthalpy change, entropy change, and Gibbs energy change"],explain:"The five classic factors affecting reaction rate are temperature, concentration, pressure (for gaseous reactions), surface area, and the presence of a catalyst, all explained via their effect on the frequency of successful collisions."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Which statement correctly distinguishes a reaction intermediate from a catalyst within a reaction mechanism?",choices:["An intermediate is produced by an earlier step in the mechanism and then consumed later, while a catalyst is present from the start and is typically regenerated, not produced, during the mechanism","An intermediate and a catalyst are the same thing, differing only in name","A catalyst is always consumed completely by the end of the mechanism, exactly like an intermediate","An intermediate always appears in the overall balanced equation, while a catalyst never does"],explain:"An intermediate arises partway through the mechanism (formed in one step, consumed in a later one); a catalyst is present at the outset of the mechanism and, while it may be consumed in one step, is regenerated by the end, so it is not a true product of the sequence."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Consider the reaction NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + CO(g) &rarr; NO(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g), with proposed mechanism step 1 (slow, RDS) NO<sub>2</sub> + NO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; NO<sub>3</sub> + NO, step 2 (fast) NO<sub>3</sub> + CO &rarr; NO<sub>2</sub> + CO<sub>2</sub>. What is the predicted rate equation?",choices:["rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>, independent of [CO]","rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>][CO], matching the overall balanced equation","rate = k[NO<sub>3</sub>][CO]","rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>][NO<sub>3</sub>][CO]"],explain:"Since the RDS (step 1) is bimolecular with respect to NO<sub>2</sub> alone, the overall rate equation is rate = k[NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>, independent of [CO] &ndash; CO only takes part in the fast step after the RDS."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In the mechanism for NO<sub>2</sub>(g) + CO(g) &rarr; NO(g) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g) above, verify that adding the two elementary steps reproduces the overall equation. What species cancels as the intermediate?",choices:["NO<sub>3</sub>, since it is formed in step 1 and consumed in step 2","NO, since it appears in both steps","CO<sub>2</sub>, since it is a final product","NO<sub>2</sub>, since it appears in both steps"],explain:"Adding step 1 (2NO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; NO<sub>3</sub> + NO) and step 2 (NO<sub>3</sub> + CO &rarr; NO<sub>2</sub> + CO<sub>2</sub>) and cancelling NO<sub>3</sub> (formed then consumed) and one NO<sub>2</sub> from each side gives the overall equation, NO<sub>2</sub> + CO &rarr; NO + CO<sub>2</sub>."},
{ch:17,q:"When investigating the effect of temperature on the reaction between HCl(aq) and magnesium, Mg(s), which variables must be controlled between trials?",choices:["The concentration of the acid and the size (surface area) of the magnesium strip","The temperature of the water bath used in each trial","The volume of gas collected in each trial","The identity of the metal used in each trial"],explain:"To isolate the effect of temperature alone, the concentration of acid and the size of the magnesium strip (surface area) must be kept identical across all trials."},
{ch:17,q:"On a graph used as evidence for experimental error, all data points are displaced consistently to one side of the expected trend line. What type of error does this indicate?",choices:["A systematic error, leading to inaccuracy in the results","A random error, leading to imprecision in the results","No error at all, since the data still forms a straight line","An error in the choice of dependent variable"],explain:"Systematic errors displace all measurements consistently above or below the expected value, producing inaccuracy (a displaced but still straight trend line)."},
{ch:17,q:"On a graph used as evidence for experimental error, data points are scattered both above and below the expected trend line with no consistent pattern. What type of error does this indicate?",choices:["A random error, leading to imprecision in the results","A systematic error, leading to inaccuracy in the results","A calibration error in the measuring equipment only","No error, since a trend line has still been drawn"],explain:"Random errors scatter points unpredictably on both sides of the trend line, due to equipment limitations, environmental changes, misread values, or too few repeats, causing imprecision."},
{ch:17,q:"The coefficient of determination, R<sup>2</sup>, is used to evaluate the fit of a trend line or curve to experimental data. What does a value of R<sup>2</sup> = 1 indicate?",choices:["The trend line or curve passes perfectly through every data point","There is no relationship at all between the two variables plotted","The reaction has reached chemical equilibrium","The rate of reaction is exactly 1 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"R<sup>2</sup> ranges from 0 to 1, with a value of 1 showing the trend line or curve passes perfectly through every data point."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: A catalyst lowers the activation energy for both the forward and reverse directions of a reversible reaction equally. Combined with the fact that a catalyst can appear in a rate equation if it participates in the RDS, which statement correctly summarizes the overall effect of a catalyst on kinetics and equilibrium?",choices:["A catalyst can change the rate equation's form (by appearing in it) and increases the rate of both forward and reverse reactions equally, but never shifts the position of equilibrium or the overall yield","A catalyst always shifts the position of equilibrium toward the products","A catalyst can never influence the mathematical form of a rate equation under any circumstances","A catalyst changes &Delta;H, which is why it can appear in the rate equation"],explain:"A catalyst may appear explicitly in the experimentally determined rate equation if it is consumed and regenerated in the mechanism, and it speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally, so that equilibrium position and yield are unaffected even though the pathway (and rate law) has changed."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For a reaction with rate equation rate = k[A], which of the following correctly completes the equation relating units: units of k = units of rate &divide; units of [A]?",choices:["s<sup>&minus;1</sup> = (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>) &divide; (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)","mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> = s<sup>&minus;1</sup> &divide; (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)","mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> dm<sup>3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> = (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>) &divide; (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)","s = (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>) &times; (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>)"],explain:"Dividing the units of rate (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>) by the units of concentration (mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup>) leaves s<sup>&minus;1</sup>, the correct units of k for a first-order reaction."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why does checking that the units of k are consistent with the given concentration and rate data provide a useful self-check in a kinetics calculation?",choices:["Because rate must always come out with units of concentration per unit time once the correct powers and units for k are used, confirming the order assumed is consistent with the data","Because units never need to balance in chemistry calculations","Because k is always dimensionless, so no check is needed","Because checking units can reveal the melting point of the reactants"],explain:"If the assumed order (and hence the form of k used) is correct, substituting the given concentration data back into the rate equation, with k's units included, must always yield units of concentration per unit time for rate &ndash; a useful check on both the order and the arithmetic."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: In a two-step reaction mechanism where the rate-determining step is the second step, how must the rate equation for that step be modified before it can represent the overall reaction?",choices:["Any intermediate concentration appearing in the rate-determining step's rate equation must be substituted using the (fast, prior) step that produces it, expressed in terms of actual reactants","No modification is ever needed; the rate equation for the final step is always the same as the overall rate equation","The rate equation must be multiplied by the number of steps in the mechanism","The concentrations of all species must be set equal to each other"],explain:"If the rate-determining step is not the first step, its rate equation will typically contain an intermediate; this intermediate's concentration must be re-expressed in terms of true reactant concentrations using the earlier (usually fast) step that forms it."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Why does the fact that reaction order must be found experimentally (rather than read off the balanced equation) matter for proposing reaction mechanisms?",choices:["Because the experimentally determined rate equation (and hence the stoichiometry of the rate-determining step) provides direct evidence that can support or rule out a proposed mechanism","Because the balanced equation is sufficient on its own to determine the full reaction mechanism","Because reaction mechanisms have no connection to the rate equation at all","Because only zero-order reactions can have a defined mechanism"],explain:"Since the rate equation reflects the stoichiometry of the rate-determining step (not necessarily the overall equation), comparing a proposed mechanism's predicted rate equation with the experimentally measured one is the main way mechanisms are tested and supported."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: For a hypothetical termolecular elementary step, 3A &rarr; products, what rate equation would collision theory predict, and why is such a step rarely proposed in practice?",choices:["rate = k[A]<sup>3</sup>; termolecular steps are rarely proposed because the simultaneous, correctly oriented, sufficiently energetic collision of three particles is statistically very unlikely","rate = k[A]; unimolecular steps are always preferred by chemists for simplicity","rate = k[A]<sup>3</sup>; termolecular steps are actually the most common type observed experimentally","rate = 3k[A]; the factor of 3 comes directly from the stoichiometric coefficient"],explain:"Collision theory predicts rate = k[A]<sup>3</sup> for a termolecular step from three A particles, but three-body collisions with simultaneous correct energy and orientation are so improbable that chemists generally propose two-step (unimolecular/bimolecular) alternatives instead."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: A reaction mechanism is proposed with a rate-determining first step. A student then doubles the concentration of a reactant that only appears in a fast step occurring after the RDS. What effect should this have on the measured initial rate?",choices:["No effect, since the rate equation (set by the RDS) does not depend on that reactant's concentration","The rate should double, since all reactants affect rate equally","The rate should quadruple, since fast steps always have second-order kinetics","The reaction should stop completely, since fast steps cannot proceed without the RDS first"],explain:"If a reactant only appears in a step after the rate-determining step, it does not appear in the rate equation, so changing its concentration should have no measurable effect on the observed initial rate &ndash; a testable prediction that supports or challenges a proposed mechanism."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Which statement correctly compares the determination of a rate equation with the determination of an equilibrium constant expression from a balanced equation?",choices:["A rate equation must be found experimentally and may not match the stoichiometric coefficients, whereas an equilibrium constant expression is written directly from the coefficients of the balanced (overall) equation","Both a rate equation and an equilibrium constant expression must always be found experimentally","Both a rate equation and an equilibrium constant expression are written directly from the balanced equation's coefficients","Neither a rate equation nor an equilibrium constant expression bears any relationship to the balanced equation"],explain:"Unlike an equilibrium constant expression, which is written directly using the coefficients of the overall balanced equation, a rate equation reflects only the stoichiometry of the rate-determining step and must be determined experimentally."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Which pair of statements about the rate constant, k, and the Arrhenius factor, A, is correct?",choices:["Both k and A share the same units for a given reaction, and both increase in magnitude as the reaction's activation energy decreases (at fixed temperature)","k and A always have different units, regardless of the reaction's order","A is always dimensionless, while k always has units of mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup>","Neither k nor A depends in any way on the activation energy of a reaction"],explain:"Since k = Ae<sup>&minus;E<sub>a</sub>/RT</sup>, A carries the same units as k (fixed by the overall order); at a given temperature, a lower E<sub>a</sub> gives a value of e<sup>&minus;E<sub>a</sub>/RT</sup> closer to 1, so for the same A, k (and hence effectively the reaction's apparent rate) is larger."},
{ch:17,q:"HL: Which of the following best explains why a reaction's rate equation can show an order of zero with respect to a reactant that is nonetheless essential to the overall balanced equation?",choices:["The reactant takes part only in a step that occurs after the rate-determining step, so changing its concentration cannot change the overall observed rate","A reactant with zero order is never actually consumed during the reaction","Zero-order reactants are always catalysts rather than true reactants","The balanced equation must be incorrect if any reactant shows zero order"],explain:"A reactant can be essential to the overall stoichiometry while being completely absent from the rate-determining step, meaning it has zero order in the rate equation even though the reaction cannot occur, overall, without it."}
]
